WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.494
Xanthan gum is the thickener that carries a lot of what we spray. So

2
00:00:03.594 --> 00:00:06.561
a fair question is, how hard is it to push through a tube?

3
00:00:07.333 --> 00:00:10.950
Its behaviour is one line. Shear stress equals a constant K times the

4
00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:14.021
strain rate raised to a power n. For xanthan n is about a

5
00:00:14.121 --> 00:00:17.577
quarter, well below one, and that is what makes it shear-thinning.

6
00:00:17.666 --> 00:00:20.557
In plain terms, the faster you push it, the thinner

7
00:00:20.657 --> 00:00:22.902
it gets. At rest it is thick, so it does

8
00:00:23.002 --> 00:00:25.951
not drip or run off. Under pressure it flows freely.

9
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:29.819
Put that law into steady laminar flow down a straight tube, and

10
00:00:29.919 --> 00:00:33.814
you get the generalized Hagen-Poiseuille result. It ties the pressure you apply

11
00:00:33.914 --> 00:00:36.089
at one end to the flow you get out the other.

12
00:00:37.166 --> 00:00:40.340
That is the equation the page solves, forwards and backwards,

13
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.702
so it can report either the flow for a given

14
00:00:42.802 --> 00:00:44.903
push or the push needed for a given flow.

15
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.922
Water it down and the exponent n climbs back toward one, so

16
00:00:49.022 --> 00:00:52.694
it behaves more like plain water, and the consistency K drops. Both

17
00:00:52.794 --> 00:00:55.735
mean it thins out and needs far less pressure to move.

18
00:00:57.733 --> 00:01:01.187
Two honest limits. Every number on the page comes straight from

19
00:01:01.287 --> 00:01:04.459
these equations, so it cannot drift from the model. But it

20
00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:08.464
is one straight, smooth, isothermal tube in laminar flow. Real delivery

21
00:01:08.564 --> 00:01:12.131
has bends, fittings and temperature swings this does not capture.

